        "Why is 'grapheme-colour synesthesia' (seeing numbers and letters in specific colours) the most common form of synesthesia?|Because in the fusiform gyrus of the brain, the area that processes the shapes of numbers/letters sits right next to the colour-processing area (V4)|Because children learn to read using coloured chalk|Because numbers are easier to pronounce than words|Because eyes have special cells for numbers",
        "What is 'synaptic pruning' during human infancy and childhood?|The process by which the growing brain trims away extra cross-connections between neighbouring sensory regions to keep the senses separate|A surgical operation performed by doctors|The growth of new teeth in children|The loss of memory in old age",
